{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Why, HaShem, should Your anger burn, etc.</b> According to the words of the Sages, their memory for blessing (Exodus Rabbah 42:6), who said that the mixed multitude alone made the calf, yet He said (Exodus 32:7): ״שִׁחֵת עַמְּךָ״ — and according to their words, their memory for blessing, it is necessarily the case that the indignation was upon all Israel because they did not protest. Accordingly, Moses' intent in saying <b>לָמָּה</b> would be an argument that is heard as follows: since the mixed multitude are the ones who corrupted, it is not fitting that His anger burn against His people, who are Israel. And even though they have judgment and justice, despite all this, not to burn His anger against them. And from the implication of the matter you know that he agreed to the destruction of the mixed multitude. And perhaps this is what he precisely intended by saying <b>אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתָ מֵאֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם</b> — and not the mixed multitude, who left of their own accord. And it appears that all the mixed multitude who were then present died, for even the patron who received them agreed to their annihilation, and also he had no standing to seek mercy upon them — since for receiving them he was punished, as we wrote above — nor do they have the merit of the fathers and the arguments that Moses invoked in his plea." "And according to the plain sense of the verse — that Israel made the calf and bowed to it — Moses argued on behalf of the portion who did not participate, for you will find that all the doers of wickedness died, and they are three categories: there are those who served with witnesses and warning, and there are those with witnesses alone, and there are those with warning alone, and all these died, and none remained except those in whom none of the three were found. And HaShem said to Moses: ״וְאֶעֱשֶׂה אוֹתְךָ לְגוֹי גָּדוֹל״ — this indicates that He sought to uproot them all. Therefore his heart cried out to HaShem: <b>לָמָּה ה׳ יֶחֱרֶה אַפְּךָ בְּעַמֶּךָ</b> — meaning, against those who did not serve at all and did not rebel against You. For on the argument of suretyship, the remembrance of עַמֶּךָ shall not be uprooted from them." "<b>Whom You brought out, etc.</b> As an argument he comes to refute what He said: <b>וְאֶעֱשֶׂה אוֹתְךָ לְגוֹי</b>, etc., for it is impossible to find the establishment of the felicity of righteousness as it is found in this people, for it was they whom He brought out of Egypt — which is not so regarding the children of Moses whom HaShem would raise up, who are not in this category. And will you not find that at the beginning of HaShem's word in the Ten Commandments (Exodus 20:2) He said: ״אֲשֶׁר הוֹצֵאתִיךָ״, etc. — this indicates that the Exodus from Egypt is the matter that establishes their bondage to the Creator. And go now and see what I have written in the commentary on that verse. Further, in all the commandments you will find an allusion to the Exodus from Egypt, and if these would not stand in the service of holiness, how much more so those who would descend from Moses, who did not experience it and are not in the category of the Exodus from Egypt. And from now on, this loss has no repair in the children of Moses. And His saying <b>בְּכֹחַ גָּדוֹל</b> — meaning, there is reason to be concerned that which came with great power might cause him loss." "<b>Further,</b> it intends His saying <b>אֲשֶׁר</b>, etc. — meaning, inasmuch as HaShem said to him <b>שִׁחֵת עַמְּךָ</b>, not wishing to designate them as His, if so, how could Moses turn to mention them before Him by the name ״עַמְּךָ״, which is something HaShem had denied? Therefore he was precise in his language and said <b>הוֹצֵאתָ</b>, etc. — meaning, not on account of their still being עַמְּךָ do I call them עַמְּךָ, but rather by this distinction אֲשֶׁר, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:36:02.321965329Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}